  "habitat": "Pairs in thornveld and broad-leaved woodland. It creeps in thickets and raises its tail when alarmed.",
  "summary": "A common, near-endemic resident. It is less white and less clearly barred below than Stierling’s Wren-Warbler, and the breast is duller in non-breeding plumage. The short identification line says it is well barred brown and has a dark breast in summer. The call is a mournful brreeet-brreeet, in bursts of three to five. No openly licensed Kruger photograph was found. No Kemp estimate is published, and that gap was left as it is.",
